        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 62,
        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: We need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 430,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 432,
        "char_start": 137,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 189,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 190,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: Since \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\) and \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\), we can combine these congruences into one. Let \\( y = x - 10 \\). Then:\n\\[ y \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ y \\equiv 0 \\pmod{13} \\]\nThis means \\( y \\) is a multiple of both 11 and 13, so \\( y = 143k \\) for some integer \\( k \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 729,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 731,
        "char_start": 434,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 344,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 345,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Substitute back to get \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 143k + 10 \\]\nNow, we need \\( x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\):\n\\[ 143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), we have:\n\\[ 6k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\] (since \\(-5 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\))"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1031,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1033,
        "char_start": 732,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 473,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 474,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: To solve for \\( k \\), we need the multiplicative inverse of 6 modulo 7. The inverse of 6 modulo 7 is 6, because \\( 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\). So:\n\\[ k \\equiv 2 \\times 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nThus, \\( k = 7m + 5 \\) for some integer \\( m \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1174,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1175,
        "char_start": 1035,
        "effective_step": 5,
        "end_token_index": 547,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 548,
        "step_number": 5,
        "text": "Step 5: Substitute \\( k \\) back into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 143(7m + 5) + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 725 + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 735 \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1387,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1388,
        "char_start": 1177,
        "effective_step": 6,
        "end_token_index": 666,
        "near_end": true,
        "prefix_token_count": 667,
        "step_number": 6,
        "text": "Step 6: We need the largest \\( x \\) less than 2010:\n\\[ 1001m + 735 < 2010 \\]\n\\[ 1001m < 1275 \\]\n\\[ m < \\frac{1275}{1001} \\approx 1.273 \\]\nThe largest integer \\( m \\) is 1. So:\n\\[ x = 1001 \\cdot 1 + 735 = 1736 \\]"
      }
    ]
