  "raw_text": "Step 1: We need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\nStep 2: Since \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\) and \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\), we can combine these congruences into one. Let \\( y = x - 10 \\). Then:\n\\[ y \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ y \\equiv 0 \\pmod{13} \\]\nThis means \\( y \\) is a multiple of both 11 and 13, so \\( y = 143k \\) for some integer \\( k \\).\n\nStep 3: Substitute back to get \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 143k + 10 \\]\nNow, we need \\( x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\):\n\\[ 143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), we have:\n\\[ 6k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\] (since \\(-5 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\))\nStep 4: To solve for \\( k \\), we need the multiplicative inverse of 6 modulo 7. The inverse of 6 modulo 7 is 6, because \\( 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\). So:\n\\[ k \\equiv 2 \\times 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nThus, \\( k = 7m + 5 \\) for some integer \\( m \\).\n\nStep 5: Substitute \\( k \\) back into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 143(7m + 5) + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 725 + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 735 \\]\n\nStep 6: We need the largest \\( x \\) less than 2010:\n\\[ 1001m + 735 < 2010 \\]\n\\[ 1001m < 1275 \\]\n\\[ m < \\frac{1275}{1001} \\approx 1.273 \\]\nThe largest integer \\( m \\) is 1. So:\n\\[ x = 1001 \\cdot 1 + 735 = 1736 \\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{1736}<|im_end|>",
